Assignments
Database Monitoring and Performance Analysis
Objective :
Gain hands-on experience in monitoring MongoDB and analyzing query performance.

Create a sample database, e.g., ecommerce.

Create a collection named products with sample documents containing the following fields:
name (String)
price (Number)
category (String)
stock (Number)

Check Database Health

Run db.serverStatus() to check MongoDB server health and general metrics.
Run db.currentOp() to monitor currently running operations.

Query and Analyze Performance

Write and execute 3–5 sample queries.
For each query, append .explain("executionStats") to analyze query execution.
Note key metrics like:
executionTimeMillis
totalDocsExamined
totalKeysExamined

Enable Profiling and Analyze Slow Queries

Enable database profiling. (db.setProfilingLevel(1))
Run various queries again to let MongoDB log them.
Find slow queries using.

Optimize Performance

Identify at least two slow queries from the profiling data.
Suggest and implement improvements (e.g., adding indexes).
Re-run the same queries with .explain("executionStats") and compare metrics.

Documentation & Deliverables

Report
Summary of server status and profiling level
Query execution metrics before and after optimization
Details of indexes added
Screenshots or logs
Output of explain("executionStats") for at least two queries (before and after)
Output of profiling results (system.profile collection)





Database Monitoring and Performance Analysis

Objective :
Practice MongoDB backup and restore techniques for effective data management and recovery.

Database and Sample Data Setup

Create a new MongoDB database (e.g., ecommerce_backup).
Add two collections:
users
orders
Insert sample data into both collections.

Logical Backup with mongoexport

Export the users collection to a JSON file

Binary Backup with mongodump

Create a binary backup of the entire database

Simulate Data Loss and Restore

Simulate a data loss by dropping the users collection
Restore from JSON using mongoimport
Restore from binary backup using mongorestore

Automate Backup with Script or Cron Job

Write a shell script (backup.sh) to automate mongodump
Schedule a daily backup using cron:

Deliverables

Exported files:
users_backup.json
Binary backup directory from mongodump
A document/report including:
Steps followed
Commands used for export, import, dump, and restore
Screenshots or logs of before and after collection drop and restoration
Any issues or learning points encountered
(Optional) Script file for automated backup (backup.sh)









User Management and Security Configuration
Objective:
Understand how to manage users, assign roles, and secure a MongoDB instance using authentication and TLS/SSL encryption.

Enable Authentication in MongoDB

Start MongoDB with authentication enabled
Restart MongoDB to apply the configuration

Create Users with Role-Based Access

Connect without auth and create the first admin user.

Authenticate as admin.

Create the following users:
Read-only user for “products” collection
Read-write user for “orders” collection

Test User Permissions

Connect as each user
Try the following actions and document results:
readUser: Try inserting into products (should fail).
writeUser: Try reading from and writing to orders (should succeed).
writeUser: Try deleting from products (should fail).
admin: Try all operations (should succeed).

Configure TLS/SSL Encryption

Generate or obtain TLS certificate and key files (self-signed or CA-issued)
Update MongoDB config (mongod.conf) to enable TLS
Restart MongoDB with the new configuration
Connect using MongoDB client with SSL

Deliverables

Documentation:
Commands for user creation and role assignments
Authentication method used (--auth or config file)
Description of which actions succeeded or failed for each user
Steps to generate and configure TLS/SSL
Screenshots:
Terminal outputs showing permission success and failure for each user
Connection over TLS/SSL (optional: include mongo --ssl output)
Configuration Snippet:
Relevant part of mongod.conf showing:

Setting Up and Managing a Replica Set
Objective:
Configure and manage a MongoDB Replica Set to ensure high availability and data redundancy.

Replica Set Setup

Set up a MongoDB Replica Set consisting of three nodes:
One primary node
One secondary node
One arbiter node
Ensure all nodes are properly networked and can communicate with each other.

Replica Set Initialization

Initialize the Replica Set.
Add the remaining nodes to the Replica Set.
Verify the status of the Replica Set after adding the nodes.

Simulate Failover and Observe Election

Simulate a failure by stopping the current primary node.
Observe the automatic election process and identify the new primary.
Restart the original primary node and observe how it rejoins the Replica Set.

Advanced Node Configuration

Configure a hidden node intended for analytics or reporting workloads.
Configure a delayed node that maintains a time-lagged copy of the primary node for recovery purposes.
Verify both nodes appear correctly in the Replica Set status.

Secure the Replica Set

Enable keyfile authentication to secure communication between Replica Set members.
Ensure only authenticated nodes can join the Replica Set.
Verify secure inter-node communication is functioning properly.

Deliverables

A step-by-step written guide describing the entire Replica Set setup process.
Logs or screenshots showing:
The election process during primary failure
The Replica Set status before and after failover
A description of how the hidden and delayed nodes were configured and their intended purpose.
Notes on any issues encountered and how they were resolved.


Sharding Implementation and Data Distribution
Objective:
Practice configuring a MongoDB sharded cluster to achieve horizontal scalability and manage large datasets effectively.

Sharded Cluster Setup

Set up a MongoDB sharded cluster with the following components:
One shard server
One config server
One query router (mongos)
Ensure all components are correctly networked and running.

Database and Data Preparation

Create a new sample database and a collection.
Populate the collection with at least 1,000 records to simulate a real-world data volume.

Enable Sharding

Choose an appropriate shard key based on the structure and access patterns of your dataset.
Enable sharding for the database and shard the chosen collection using the selected shard key.

Verify and Monitor Sharding

Insert additional data into the sharded collection.
Use appropriate monitoring tools or commands to verify that data is being distributed across shards.
Review the current state of the cluster and data distribution.

Implement Tag-Aware Sharding

Configure zones/tags to associate specific data subsets (e.g., based on region or other criteria) with specific shards.
Configure shard zone ranges to route the appropriate data to the corresponding shard.
Verify that tag-aware sharding is working as intended by monitoring the data placement.

Deliverables

A report that includes:
The complete sharded cluster setup process
The rationale behind the chosen shard key
Observations on how data was distributed across shards
Explanation of the tag-aware sharding configuration
Screenshots showing:
Cluster status and shard information
Output verifying data distribution
Tag-aware sharding configuration and its effect



Optimizing MongoDB Configuration for Performance
Objective:
Analyze and modify MongoDB server configuration to improve performance under a specific workload scenario.

Setup Environment

Set up a standalone MongoDB instance.
Create and populate a sample dataset appropriate for a real-world use case (e.g., e-commerce).
Ensure collections reflect the intended workload type (e.g., read-heavy or write-heavy).

Configuration Tuning

Identify and modify at least three performance-related parameters in the MongoDB configuration file.
Adjust the settings based on the chosen workload characteristics.
Document each configuration change and the reasoning behind it.

Performance Testing

Design a set of sample queries or operations representative of the workload.
Run the test queries before applying configuration changes and record performance metrics.
Apply the new configuration and restart the MongoDB instance.
Re-run the same test queries and record updated performance metrics.

Resource Monitoring

Use monitoring tools such as mongostat or mongotop to track resource usage during query execution.
Record system behavior, including memory usage, I/O activity, and operation throughput.

Deliverables

A modified MongoDB configuration file with comments clearly explaining the purpose of each change.
A report that includes:
Description of the initial workload scenario
Configuration changes made and their rationale
Performance comparison before and after optimization
Output and analysis from monitoring tools used













